<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip of Milly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1171</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of Milly was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> noble of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, who exchanged it in 1161 for the lordship of Oultrejordain with its castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and Montréal. He became Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> in 1169 and died in 1171. Oultrejordain passed through his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanie-of-milly">Stephanie of Milly</a> to her husbands, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-of-milly.jpg" alt="Philip of Milly" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Philippe_de_Milly.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
